# Passwordless input lock control for administrators.
#
# Installed to /etc/sudoers.d/input-lock-x (mode 0440), and validated with
# `visudo -c` before installation. Skip it with: ./install.sh --no-sudoers
#
# Why this exists: `ssh laptop input-lock-x-emergency-unlock` allocates no TTY,
# so sudo cannot prompt for a password -- and that command is the documented way
# back in when the keyboard is disabled. Without this the emergency path fails
# at the moment it is needed.
#
# The scope is two root-owned scripts that only start/stop one systemd unit and
# clear input inhibit flags. It is the same privilege the polkit rule already
# grants an active local session; this extends it to SSH.
#
# Three group names because distributions disagree -- see the polkit rule for
# the same list. A group that does not exist never matches.
#
# To opt out: delete this file. The scripts still work as
#   ssh -t <host> sudo input-lock-x-emergency-unlock

%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x-emergency-unlock, /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x
%sudo  ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x-emergency-unlock, /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x
%admin ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x-emergency-unlock, /usr/local/bin/input-lock-x
